          "criterion": "ma_vs_federal_distinctions",
          "location": "orientation[6]; ma_vs_federal[0]; decision_logic[8]; retrieval_prompts[16].answer; against worked_examples[5].steps[1]",
          "problem": "Four places state the federal PREP Act coverage for technician-administered seasonal influenza vaccine as 'persons aged three or older', while worked_examples[5].steps[1] states it as 'persons aged 19 and older'. The section does not explain that the Declaration reaches the 'three or older' result through two different limbs rather than one figure.",
          "rationale": "Both figures are correct but they come from different clauses. Section V(d)(2) of the Declaration, the clause that names qualified pharmacy technicians and seasonal influenza expressly, reaches persons 'aged 19 and older'; ages three through 18 are reached through the childhood limb, section V(d)(1), which section XII(i)ii confirms by extending liability protection to technician-administered 'CDC/ACIP recommended seasonal influenza vaccines for persons aged three through 18' through December 31, 2029, alongside section XII(i)iv for 'individuals aged nineteen and above'. The aggregate 'three or older' is therefore an accurate net statement of coverage and the worked example is accurate for its adult clinic, so no candidate is taught a wrong rule; the unexplained mismatch between the two figures is a presentational imprecision, weighed as Low. The COVID-19 age ('three or older'), the technician conditions and the December 31, 2029 end date are all stated correctly.",
